---
// What the site records about visits, in plain words. Linked from the footer.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/base';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="What this site records about visits, and how to opt out.">
	<article class="privacy">
		<h1>Privacy</h1>
		<p class="dek">Short version: I see how people find and use this site, not who they are.</p>

		<h2>What's recorded</h2>
		<p>
			This site uses PostHog, hosted in the EU, to understand visits. For each visit it records the pages viewed, how you
			arrived (the site that sent you, or a tag in a link I shared), clicks on the résumé, contact and demo links, your
			browser and device type, and an approximate location (country and city) worked out from your IP address.
		</p>
		<p>
			Your IP address is stored with those events. I use it to see which organization's network a visit came from,
			where that's possible, which mostly works for large employers' office networks. I don't try to identify
			individual people.
		</p>

		<h2>What isn't</h2>
		<p>
			No cookies and no browser storage: visits are tied together by an anonymous hash on PostHog's side. No session
			recordings, no advertising trackers, and nothing is sold or shared.
		</p>

		<h2>Opting out</h2>
		<p>
			If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, nothing is recorded at all. To have data from your
			visits deleted, get in touch through the <a href={withBase('#contact')}>contact section</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Contact details</h2>
		<p>
			To keep my phone number and email away from scrapers, they may appear only after a quick check by Cloudflare
			Turnstile, which looks at browser signals to tell people from bots. That check is covered by Cloudflare's own
			privacy policy.
		</p>

		<h2>Demos</h2>
		<p>The coded-project demos run on their own hosting and don't share data with this site.</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.privacy {
		max-width: 44rem;
	}

	.dek {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-size: var(--text-lg);
	}

	.privacy h2 {
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
		margin-top: var(--space-5);
	}

	.privacy p {
		margin: var(--space-3) 0;
		line-height: var(--leading-prose);
	}
</style>
